 ## Overview
-Two-stage ray marching is an acceleration technique that splits ray traversal into a cheap "Stage 1" occupancy lookup phase and an expensive "Stage 2" density sampling phase [S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of evaluating complex density functions in empty space by skipping entire volume blocks identified as empty by a lower-resolution occupancy map [S1]. Unlike distance map methods that provide precise exit distances, this method uses binary occupancy flags to jump between blocks, trading precision for simplicity and low memory overhead [S1]. It sets itself apart from hierarchical sampling by using a flat, uniform occupancy grid rather than a tree structure, and from training-time bounding boxes by performing skipping at runtime based on precomputed block status [S1][S2, unofficial].
+Two-stage Ray Marching with Empty-Space Skipping is a rendering optimization that accelerates volumetric ray casting by decoupling the search for active volume data from the expensive density sampling process [S1][S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of wasting computational resources sampling empty space by using a lower-resolution occupancy map to quickly identify entry points into active regions [S1][S2, unofficial]. Unlike its siblings, it employs a strict two-phase loop: Stage 1 performs cheap, coarse-grained occupancy lookups to skip inactive blocks, while Stage 2 performs fine-grained, expensive density sampling only within confirmed active regions [S2, unofficial]. This approach is distinct from hierarchical methods like [Hierarchical Sampling with Occupancy Grid](3035-hierarchical-sampling-with-occupancy-grid.md) by using a flat occupancy map and fixed-step skipping rather than recursive tree traversal, and from [Distance Map Acceleration](972-distance-map-acceleration.md) by relying on binary occupancy flags rather than distance-to-nearest-occupied-voxel metrics [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Inputs:** High-resolution volume data (density field), occupancy map (binary grid), camera parameters (origin, direction), block size factor $B$ (ratio of volume resolution to occupancy map resolution) [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Outputs:** Final pixel color (composited density and color) [S2, unofficial].
-
